UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Walking is one of the simplest forms of exercise there is. You do not need a gym membership, special equipment, or previous experience. You already know how to do it. All you need is a pair of comfortable shoes and a little time in your day. For many people, walking is the easiest way to move from doing almost no exercise to being regularly active.</p><p>This guide explains how much walking actually helps, how to start safely if you are new to exercise, and how to turn a short daily walk into a habit that lasts. There is no single perfect amount of walking, and there is no need to chase a magic number. The goal is to move more than you did yesterday, and to keep doing it.</p><h2>How Much Walking Helps</h2><p>Walking is a gentle form of exercise that uses your legs, your core, and your arms. A steady walk raises your heart rate a little, warms up your joints, and gets your blood moving. Over time, regular walking can help you feel less stiff in the morning, sleep better at night, and have more energy during the day. Many people also find that a walk clears their head and lifts their mood, especially after a long day sitting at a desk.</p><p>Walking supports your general fitness without putting heavy strain on your knees and ankles. Because one foot is always on the ground, the impact is much lower than running or jumping. That makes it a good choice for people returning to exercise after a long break, for older adults, and for anyone carrying extra weight. It is also easy to fit into a normal day. You can walk to the shop, walk part of the way to work, or take a short walk after lunch. Even ten minutes of walking is better than ten minutes of sitting still, and short walks add up. Three ten-minute walks give you thirty minutes of movement, a solid daily target for a beginner.</p><h2>How to Start Safely</h2><p>If you have not exercised for a long time, start slowly. Begin with short, easy walks on flat ground, for about ten to fifteen minutes at a time. Walk at a pace where you can still talk comfortably. If you are breathing so hard that you cannot finish a sentence, slow down. There is no prize for going too fast on day one, and pushing too hard is the fastest way to feel sore, tired, and discouraged.</p><p>Wear comfortable shoes with good support and socks that do not rub. Loose, comfortable clothing helps you move freely. In warm weather, take water with you and walk in the shade when you can. In cold weather, wear layers you can remove as you warm up. Walk on even surfaces at first, such as pavements and park paths, and watch out for uneven ground, loose stones, and traffic. Start slowly for the first few minutes, settle into your normal pace, and slow down again for the last few minutes instead of stopping suddenly.</p><h3>Listen to Your Body</h3><p>Some mild muscle tiredness after a new activity is normal, but sharp pain is not. This article is general education, not medical advice. Stop walking and rest if you feel pain, dizziness, chest discomfort, or unusual breathlessness. If you have a health condition, such as heart trouble, diabetes, joint problems, or high blood pressure, or if you have been told to be careful with exercise, talk to your doctor before starting a new walking routine. The same applies if you are recovering from an injury or surgery.</p><h2>Building a Daily Habit</h2><p>The hardest part of walking is not the walking itself. It is remembering to do it, day after day. The best way to build the habit is to attach your walk to something you already do. Walk after breakfast, walk after lunch, or walk in the evening after dinner. When the walk has a fixed place in your routine, you do not have to decide each day whether to go. You just go.</p><p>Start with a goal you know you can meet. Five days a week for fifteen minutes is a good first target. If that feels easy after two weeks, add five minutes, or add one more day. Small increases are safer than sudden jumps, and they are easier to keep. If you miss a day, do not treat it as a failure. Just walk the next day. One missed walk does not undo the habit, so get back on track quickly and kindly.</p><p>Walking with someone else can help you stay consistent. A friend, family member, or neighbour makes the time pass faster and gives you a reason to show up. If you prefer to walk alone, music or a podcast can make the walk more enjoyable. Some people like to keep a simple record of their walks, such as the date and how long they walked. You do not have to set aside a special hour to walk more. Get off the bus one stop early, take the stairs for one or two floors, or walk to a nearby shop instead of driving. A short walk on a rainy day is still a walk, and it still counts. Start small, walk regularly, and let the habit grow at your own pace.</p>' WHERE id = 21;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Learning a new digital skill can feel like a huge task, but it does not have to be. You do not need to study for hours every day, and you do not need to pay for an expensive course. With one skill, thirty days, and thirty minutes a day, you can make real progress. Fifteen hours of focused practice is enough to go from knowing nothing to doing useful, basic work.</p><p>This guide gives you a realistic plan. It works for many digital skills, such as spreadsheets, photo editing, basic coding, video editing, or graphic design. The steps are the same whatever you choose. Pick one skill, learn a little each day, practise what you learn, and finish the month with something real to show for it.</p><h2>Step One: Pick One Skill</h2><p>Choose a single skill and write it down. The skill should be specific enough that you can tell whether you are improving. Learning spreadsheets is vague. Learning to build a simple budget spreadsheet with formulas is specific. A clear target makes it much easier to plan your days and to see your progress.</p><p>Pick a skill that connects to something you actually want to do. If you want to organise your money better, learn spreadsheets. If you want to make simple posters for a club or a small business, learn a basic design tool. Motivation lasts longer when the skill solves a real problem in your life.</p><p>Before you start, check what you need. Most digital skills only need a computer or a phone, an internet connection, and a free tool. Many good tools have free versions that are enough for a beginner. Do not spend money on day one. Start free, and only think about paying later, when you know the skill is worth it.</p><h2>The Thirty-Day Plan</h2><p>The plan has four weeks, and each week has a different job. You will spend about thirty minutes a day, five or six days a week, with one rest day. Thirty minutes is short enough to fit into a busy day and long enough to learn something real. A short session is far better than skipping the day entirely.</p><h3>Week One: Learn the Basics (Days 1 to 7)</h3><p>Your job this week is to learn the layout of your tool and the most common actions. Follow a beginner tutorial with your own copy of the tool open. Learn how to open a file, save your work, undo a mistake, and find the main menus. By the end of the week, you should be able to do five or six basic actions without looking them up every time.</p><h3>Week Two: Practise Small Tasks (Days 8 to 14)</h3><p>This week, stop following tutorials step by step and start doing small tasks on your own. If you are learning spreadsheets, build a small weekly shopping list with totals. If you are learning design, make a simple poster. These tasks will feel slow, and you will need to look things up.</p><h3>Week Three: Build a Real Project (Days 15 to 21)</h3><p>Now pick one real project and work on it a little each day. The project should be something you would actually use or show to someone: a budget tracker, a short video about a place you like, or a simple web page about a hobby. A project gives your practice a purpose and forces you to solve real problems.</p><h3>Week Four: Finish and Review (Days 22 to 30)</h3><p>Use this week to finish your project, tidy it up, and fix the mistakes you noticed. Then look back at what you learned. Write down the ten most useful things you can now do, and try each one from memory. On the last day, show your project to someone and explain how you made it.</p><h2>Free Resources You Can Use</h2><p>There are many free ways to learn a digital skill. Free video lessons on large video platforms can walk you through the basics. Public libraries often give free access to online learning courses. The help pages published by the makers of a tool are usually free. Online forums can help when you get stuck, if you describe your problem clearly.</p><p>When you choose a resource, check that it matches your tool and your level. A tutorial for a different version can be confusing, and a course for experts will move too fast. Pick one main resource and stick with it. One finished course teaches more than five half-finished ones.</p><h2>How to Stay on Track</h2><p>Keep your practice time fixed. The same thirty minutes each day, if you can manage it, makes the habit easier to keep. Put your phone on silent during practice, and keep a notebook next to you for notes and questions. Track your progress simply: a calendar with a tick for each day you practised is enough.</p><p>Expect the middle of the month to feel hard. Around day twelve or thirteen, the newness wears off and the work feels slower. This is the point where most people stop. Push through it by making the task smaller: practise for just ten minutes if that is all you can face. Ten minutes keeps the habit alive, and you will often find that once you start, you keep going.</p>' WHERE id = 22;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>You do not need a gym, weights, or machines to exercise at home. Your own body weight is enough to build strength, improve balance, and get your heart working. A home workout can take as little as fifteen to twenty minutes, it costs nothing, and you can do it in a small space, such as a living room or a bedroom with the furniture pushed back.</p><p>This guide covers the basic bodyweight exercises, a simple weekly routine you can follow, and how to do each movement safely. You do not need to be fit to start. You only need to start at a level that suits you now and build up slowly.</p><h2>Before You Start</h2><p>Clear a space where you can stretch out your arms and lie down without hitting anything. Wear comfortable clothes and shoes with good grip, or go barefoot on a non-slip floor if you prefer. Keep a bottle of water nearby. If you have a mat, a towel, or a rug, use it for floor exercises so your knees and back are comfortable.</p><p>Always warm up first. A warm-up prepares your muscles and joints and lowers the chance of pulling something. March in place for one minute, then do slow arm circles, gentle side bends, and a few easy squats. Your warm-up should take about three to five minutes and should leave you feeling warm, not tired.</p><h3>A Note on Safety</h3><p>This article is general education, not medical advice. Stop exercising and rest if you feel pain, dizziness, chest discomfort, or unusual breathlessness. If you have a health condition, such as heart trouble, high blood pressure, diabetes, joint problems, or back trouble, or if you are recovering from an injury, talk to your doctor before starting a new exercise routine. Never push through sharp pain.</p><h2>The Basic Exercises</h2><p>These four exercises cover the main parts of your body. Learn the form first, before you worry about doing many repetitions. Good form matters more than big numbers.</p><h3>1. Squats</h3><p>Stand with your feet about shoulder-width apart. Keep your chest up and your arms out in front of you for balance. Bend your knees and push your hips back. Go down as far as you can while keeping your heels on the floor and your knees pointing in the same direction as your toes. Then push through your heels to stand back up. Do eight to twelve repetitions.</p><h3>2. Knee Push-Ups</h3><p>Start on your hands and knees, with your hands under your shoulders and your knees on the floor. Keep your body in a straight line from your head to your knees. Lower your chest towards the floor by bending your elbows, then push back up. Keep your back flat and do not let your hips sag. Do five to ten repetitions.</p><h3>3. Glute Bridge</h3><p>Lie on your back with your knees bent and your feet flat on the floor, close to your hips. Keep your arms by your sides. Push through your heels to lift your hips up until your body forms a straight line from your shoulders to your knees. Hold for a second, then lower slowly. Do ten to fifteen repetitions.</p><h3>4. Plank</h3><p>Lie face down, then lift your body onto your forearms and your toes. Keep your body in one straight line from head to heels. Do not let your hips drop or rise too high. Look at the floor and breathe normally. Hold for ten to twenty seconds to start, and rest if your form breaks.</p><h2>A Simple Weekly Routine</h2><p>Do this routine three days a week, with at least one rest day between sessions. A rest day does not mean sitting still all day. It means giving your muscles time to recover while you walk, stretch, or do light activity.</p><ul><li><strong>Warm-up:</strong> three to five minutes of marching in place, arm circles, and easy squats.</li><li><strong>Squats:</strong> two sets of eight to twelve repetitions, resting one minute between sets.</li><li><strong>Knee push-ups:</strong> two sets of five to ten repetitions.</li><li><strong>Glute bridge:</strong> two sets of ten to fifteen repetitions.</li><li><strong>Plank:</strong> two holds of ten to twenty seconds.</li><li><strong>Finish:</strong> one minute of marching in place, then gentle stretching.</li></ul><p>The whole routine takes about twenty minutes. If you are new to exercise, start with one set of each exercise instead of two. When the routine feels easier after two or three weeks, add a few more repetitions, or add a second set.</p><h2>Safe Form, Every Time</h2><p>Form is what keeps exercise safe. Move slowly and with control, especially when lowering your body. Never bounce, jerk, or rush a movement. Breathe out as you push or lift, and breathe in as you lower or return. In squats, your knees should follow the direction of your toes. If an exercise hurts a joint, stop it and try an easier version.</p><h2>Staying With It</h2><p>Pick fixed days and a fixed time for your workouts, and write them on a calendar. Three short sessions a week are enough to make progress, and a fixed schedule makes it harder to forget. Keep a simple record of what you did: the date, the exercises, and how many repetitions you managed. Watching those numbers grow slowly is one of the best rewards for sticking with it.</p>' WHERE id = 23;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>A loan lets you borrow money now and pay it back later, usually in monthly payments. The price of borrowing is interest. Interest is the extra money you pay on top of the amount you borrowed, and it is the part of a loan that most people find confusing.</p><p>This guide explains simple interest, compound interest, and monthly payments in plain words. It includes a worked example that is clearly labelled as an illustration, not a real offer. Every example here uses round numbers to make the maths easy to follow. Real loans use different rates and terms.</p><h2>What Interest Is</h2><p>When you borrow money, the lender charges you for the use of that money. That charge is interest. It is usually shown as a yearly rate, called a percentage. A rate of ten percent a year means that for every one hundred units of money you borrow, you pay about ten units of interest in a year, if nothing else changes. The amount you borrow at the start is called the principal. The total you pay back is the principal plus the interest.</p><p>Interest is not the only cost of a loan. Some loans also have fees, such as a fee for setting up the loan or a fee for paying it back late. When you compare loans, look at the total amount you will pay, including fees, not just the interest rate.</p><h2>Simple Interest</h2><p>Simple interest is worked out only on the principal, the amount you first borrowed. The interest does not change as you pay the loan back, because it is always calculated on the same starting amount. You borrow a fixed amount, and you pay a fixed amount of interest each year on that amount. If you borrow for two years, you pay two years of interest.</p><h2>Compound Interest</h2><p>Compound interest is different. It is worked out not only on the principal, but also on the interest that has already been added. Each time interest is added, the amount you owe grows, and the next lot of interest is calculated on that larger amount.</p><p>Most long-term loans, such as home loans and many personal loans, use compound interest, usually calculated monthly. Every month, interest is added to what you owe, and the next month''s interest is calculated on the new balance. If you pay on time, your payments cover the interest and slowly reduce the principal. If you miss payments, the balance can grow.</p><h2>Monthly Payments</h2><p>Most loans are repaid in monthly payments. Each payment usually covers two things. Part of it pays the interest that has built up that month. The rest reduces the principal. Early in a loan, most of your payment goes towards interest, because the balance is still large. Later, as the balance falls, more of your payment goes towards the principal.</p><p>The size of your monthly payment depends on three things: how much you borrow, the interest rate, and how long you take to pay it back. Taking longer lowers the monthly payment, because the cost is spread over more months, but it usually raises the total amount you pay, because interest is charged for a longer time.</p><h2>A Worked Example</h2><p>The example below is an illustration only. It uses round numbers to show the steps clearly. It is not a real loan offer, and real rates, fees, and terms will vary. This article is general education, not personal financial advice.</p><p><strong>Illustrative example:</strong> suppose you borrow 1,000 units for one year at a simple interest rate of ten percent a year. First, work out the interest. Ten percent of 1,000 is 100. So the interest for the year is 100. Next, add the interest to the principal. The total to repay is 1,000 plus 100, which is 1,100. If you repay in twelve equal monthly payments, divide 1,100 by 12. The monthly payment is about 91.70.</p><p>Now suppose the same loan used compound interest, added monthly at the same yearly rate. The monthly rate would be roughly ten percent divided by twelve, which is about 0.83 percent a month. In the first month, interest of about 8.30 is added to the 1,000, making the balance about 1,008.30. Month by month, the interest is worked out on a slightly larger balance.</p><p>This example shows two key lessons. A longer loan usually costs more in total, even when the monthly payment is smaller. Compound interest costs more than simple interest at the same rate, because interest is charged on interest as well as on the principal.</p><h2>Before You Borrow</h2><p>Ask yourself four questions before you take a loan. First, do I need to borrow, or can I save for this instead? Second, can I afford the monthly payment every month, even if my income drops a little? Third, what is the total amount I will repay, including all fees? Fourth, what happens if I miss a payment?</p><p>Always read the loan terms in full before you sign. Check the interest rate, whether it is simple or compound, how often interest is added, the length of the loan, the monthly payment, any fees, and the total repayment. If any part is unclear, ask the lender to explain it in plain words.</p>' WHERE id = 24;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>A road trip is one of the most flexible ways to travel. You choose the route, you choose where to stop, and you can change your plans as you go. But a good road trip does not happen by accident. It takes a little planning: a sensible route, a check of the car, a plan for fuel, places to stay, food, and a clear idea of what it will cost.</p><p>The trip in the examples here is a four-day journey for two people, covering about one thousand kilometres in total.</p><h2>Plan the Route</h2><p>Start by choosing your destination and the road you will take to get there. Look at a map and work out the total distance, then divide it into daily driving sections. Most people are comfortable driving for about four to six hours a day, with breaks. Driving for much longer than that in one day is tiring, and tired driving is dangerous.</p><p>Mark the places where you will stop for fuel, food, and rest. On a long route, know where the fuel stations are, especially on quiet roads where they can be far apart. Keep a paper map or a downloaded offline map as a backup, in case your phone loses signal. Tell someone your plan: give a family member or a friend your route, your overnight stops, and your travel days.</p><h2>Check the Car Before You Go</h2><p>A few checks before you leave can prevent most roadside problems. Check the engine oil, the coolant, the brake fluid, and the windscreen washer fluid. Check the tyre pressure, including the spare tyre, and look at the tyres for cuts, cracks, or worn tread. Check that all the lights work, including brake lights and indicators. Test the brakes on a quiet road and listen for unusual noises.</p><p>Make sure the car documents are in order, and that you have the number for roadside help saved on your phone. Pack a small emergency kit: a torch, a first-aid kit, a warning triangle, a high-visibility vest, a blanket, drinking water, and a phone charger that works in the car.</p><h2>Fuel, Stays, and Food</h2><p>Fuel is usually the biggest cost of a road trip. To estimate it, divide your total distance by the distance your car travels on one unit of fuel, then multiply by the price of fuel. For example, if your car travels fifteen kilometres on one litre, and your trip is one thousand kilometres, you will need about sixty-seven litres.</p><p>For overnight stays, decide in advance whether you will book ahead or find a place when you arrive. Booking ahead gives you certainty, especially in busy seasons and in popular places. Finding a place on the day gives you freedom to change your route, but it can mean higher prices or no rooms.</p><h2>Staying Safe on the Road</h2><p>Share the driving if you can. Take a break every two hours, even if you do not feel tired. Get out of the car, stretch your legs, and drink some water. Do not drive if you are sleepy. It is far better to stop early and rest than to push on and risk an accident.</p><p>Keep your valuables out of sight when you leave the car, and lock the car every time. Park in well-lit places at night. Keep your phone charged, and carry some cash as well as a card, because some small fuel stations and shops may not accept cards. If your car breaks down, move to a safe place away from traffic, switch on your hazard lights, and call for help.</p><h2>The Complete Checklist</h2><ul><li><strong>Route:</strong> destination, daily driving sections, fuel stops, overnight addresses, offline map.</li><li><strong>Car:</strong> oil, coolant, brake fluid, washer fluid, tyre pressure and spare tyre, lights, brakes.</li><li><strong>Documents:</strong> driving licence, car papers, insurance, roadside help number.</li><li><strong>Emergency kit:</strong> torch, first-aid kit, warning triangle, high-visibility vest, blanket, water.</li><li><strong>Stays:</strong> bookings confirmed, cancellation terms checked, contact numbers saved.</li><li><strong>Food and water:</strong> snacks, drinking water, a plan for one proper meal a day.</li><li><strong>Money:</strong> card, some cash, a rough daily budget written down.</li><li><strong>Phone:</strong> charger, offline map, emergency numbers, someone at home told your plan.</li><li><strong>Comfort:</strong> sunglasses, sunscreen, warm layer, rubbish bag, tissues.</li></ul><h2>Example Budget</h2><p>The budget below is an illustrative example only. It is not a list of real prices. Prices for fuel, rooms, and food vary by place, season, and country, and you should replace every number with the price you find for your own trip. The example is for two people, four days, and about one thousand kilometres of driving.</p><p><strong>Illustrative example budget:</strong> fuel for one thousand kilometres, about 250 units. Three nights of simple lodging, about 180 units. Food and drink for two people for four days, about 160 units. Road tolls and parking, about 40 units. Snacks, water, and small supplies, about 30 units. Emergency money set aside for unexpected costs, about 50 units. The total for this example is about 710 units.</p><p>Notice that the example sets aside emergency money. A flat tyre, a longer route, or a night in a more expensive town can all add cost, and a small reserve stops a surprise from ruining the trip. A good rule is to add about ten percent to your estimated total as your reserve. Plan it well, drive safely, and enjoy the road.</p>' WHERE id = 25;
